# Research, Humor, and the Play Drive: an open-mic night with two hecklers.
# Source: blog/posts/2026-08-27-untitled.md. ¶ numbers as @read assigns them:
# ¶1 the Schiller epigraph, ¶2 "When I think about research", ¶3 the joke as
# experiment, ¶4 the fear of not knowing enough, ¶5 Schiller's drives,
# ¶6 language models, ¶7 Eisenstein and montage.
# Everything without a chip is frame fiction.
@title Research, Humor, and the Play Drive
@kind blog
@source blog:untitled
@link ../../#/post/untitled Read the post
@note The frame is fiction: the basement, the rain, the leak, the jokes, the classroom memory, the two hecklers and the understudy. Form and Sense are personified drives, not people, and the understudy is a figure for a kind of system, not any product. Lines marked ¶ are quoted, lightly trimmed, from the post; the Schiller sentence is quoted as the post quotes it.
@status published
@palette ochre
@cast Jack hue=28 skin=2 glasses short hoodie
@cast Form hue=250 none uniform skin=1
@cast Sense hue=10 curly hoodie skin=4
@cast Understudy name="the understudy" lattice=dense hue=192
@cast Schiller page name="Friedrich Schiller, Letters on the Aesthetic Education of Man"

# ------------------------------------------------------------ Prologue

@bg void
@tone night
@mode nvl
@pause 600
There is a list by the door of the basement, and my name is fourth on it.
Fourth is a bad number. The room has stopped being polite and has not yet started being drunk.
I have five minutes, a notebook, and a theory. The theory is the problem.
@page
When I think about research from a meta perspective, I keep returning to play. ^¶2
That is a fine thing to keep returning to at a desk. Tonight I have been asked to return to it out loud.
@mode adv
@pause 400
@show Schiller center
Schiller: Man only plays when in the full meaning of the word he is a man, and he is only completely a man when he plays. ^¶1
@hide Schiller
@pause 500

# ------------------------------------------------------------ Chapter 1

@chapter 1 Fourth on the List
@transition fade 1200
@bg cafe dim
@tone dusk
@fx rain on
@cg window-rain | The basement window, at street level
The window is level with the pavement. All evening I have watched the rain arrive as ankles.
Somewhere above the stage the ceiling has a leak. One drop, every so often, onto the floor beside the microphone. Nobody has put a bucket under it. It is that kind of room.
@cg off
@fx rain off
@show Jack left (thinking)
Jack: Again. From the top.
@mode nvl
A joke is an empirical experiment. ^¶3
Incongruity theory frames humor as establishing a structured expectation and abruptly breaking it. ^¶3
You cannot reliably deduce whether a punchline works in isolation. One must be a standup comic, with a live audience. ^¶3
@page
I wrote that at a desk, in daylight, with nobody watching. It was very convincing there.
Most attempts fail, the timing has to be exact, and the payoff comes from a sudden, unexpected collision of concepts. ^¶3
(Most attempts fail. I wrote that too. I did not think it meant me.)
@mode adv
@show Form center (deadpan) far
@show Sense right (neutral) far
At the front table sit two people who have not laughed all night. One has a notebook of his own and is keeping score in it. The other has taken her shoes off.
I do not know their names. By the end of the night I had given them some.
@hide all
@transition wipe-left 800
@cg corridor-light | From the bar to the stage
The walk to the microphone is eleven steps. I counted them at the sound check, when the room was empty and everything I said was funny.
Two jokes in my pocket. One I have told before, to people who love me. One I have never said aloud.
* Open with the one that has worked before. -> open_safe
* Open with the one nobody has heard. -> open_risky

== open_safe
@set open = safe
@cg off
@show Jack center (smile)
Jack: I asked the librarian where the books on paranoia were. She whispered: they're right behind you.
@pause 700
It lands the way a form lands on a desk. Somebody nods. A nod is the sound a joke makes when it has been recognised.
(It is not mine. Everyone here has met it before. That was the appeal.)
-> ch1_out

== open_risky
@set open = risky
@cg off
@show Jack center (smile)
Jack: A hypothesis is a joke with a longer set-up. You spend six months on the premise, and then the data doesn't laugh.
@pause 700
Silence. Not a hostile one. The silence of people waiting for the part where it is funny.
(It was funny in the notebook. The notebook has no face.)
-> ch1_out

== ch1_out
@show Form left (deadpan)
Form: Structure.
@show Sense right (deadpan)
Sense: I didn't feel anything.
@fx shake
Drip. Beside the microphone, exactly on the beat I had left for the laugh.
Jack (worried): Thank you. I'm here for four more minutes.
(I was in a room once where nothing could go wrong. I remember it better than I would like.)
@hide all
@pause 600

# ------------------------------------------------------------ Chapter 2

@chapter 2 Predictable Steps
@fx none
@fx flash
@flashback on A classroom, some years earlier
@transition white 900
@bg classroom
@tone memory
@fx dust on
Chalk dust in a bar of afternoon light. A proof going up on the board, one line under another, each line the only thing the line above could have become.
I loved it. I want that on the record. Nobody in that room could bomb.
In a classroom, you follow established proofs and predictable steps. ^¶3
@show Jack right (neutral) far
I sat near the window and copied down every line. At the end there was a small black square, and the square meant it had worked. It had worked before any of us were born.
@hide Jack
@transition dissolve 700
@cg page | Notes, every line following from the last
@mode nvl
The common fear starting out is that you simply do not know enough. ^¶4
I had that fear in its pure form. I thought there was a shelf somewhere, and that when I had read to the end of the shelf I would be allowed to begin.
So I copied. Copying felt like approaching.
@page
The real requirement is taste, curiosity, and a willingness to tinker. ^¶4
The bottleneck is not passive knowledge accumulation, but active iteration. ^¶4
Nobody says this in the room with the chalk, because in that room it is not true. In that room the punchline is printed at the bottom of the page.
@page
With research, you have to generate hypotheses, test the absurd ones, and tolerate a high failure rate to find what lands. ^¶3
*What lands.* I chose that verb at a desk. It is a verb from this basement.
@mode adv
@cg off
@fx dust off
@flashback off

# ------------------------------------------------------------ Chapter 3

@chapter 3 Two Hecklers
@transition dissolve 900
@bg cafe dim
@tone dusk
@show Jack center (worried)
Three and a half minutes. The front table has decided to help.
@show Form left (deadpan)
Form: You had a set-up and a turn. The turn did not follow from the set-up. I checked.
@show Sense right (puzzled)
Sense: He's checking. Listen to him. Nobody ever laughed because it followed.
Form (deadpan): Nobody ever laughed because it was damp, either.
I had read about these two. I had not expected them to buy tickets.
@mode nvl
In the Aesthetic Letters, Schiller introduces the play-drive (*Spieltrieb*) as the necessary mediator between two opposing forces. ^¶5
The form-drive (*Formtrieb*), which seeks abstraction, logic, and order. ^¶5
And the sense-drive (*Stofftrieb*), which is rooted in raw material, sensation, and time. ^¶5
@page
On the page they are two nouns with a hyphen each. In a basement they have elbows.
@mode adv
@set asked = 0

== heckle
* (once) Answer the one with the notebook. -> to_form
* (once) Answer the one with no shoes. -> to_sense
* (if asked == 2) Stop answering them. -> heckle_done

== to_form
@add asked 1
Jack (neutral): You want it to follow.
Form (thinking): I want to know, before you open your mouth, that it will work. Give me the premise and I will derive the laugh.
Jack (deadpan): And if you can derive it?
Form (deadpan): Then I have no need to laugh. I already have it.
(He is everything I trusted in the room with the chalk. He would have given my proof full marks and gone home.)
-> heckle

== to_sense
@add asked 1
Jack (neutral): You want to feel something.
Sense (smile): I want the chair to be hard and the beer to be warm and your voice to crack. I want it to be *now*.
Jack (puzzled): A voice cracking isn't a joke.
Sense (laugh): No. It's just a Thursday. But it's a real one.
(She would laugh at a man falling down the stairs and not ask what it meant. She is not wrong that he fell.)
-> heckle

== heckle_done
Jack (thinking): Neither of you has laughed once tonight.
Form (deadpan): Nothing surprised me.
Sense (deadpan): Everything surprised me. None of it meant to.
Play happens only when these two impulses collide and balance each other out. ^¶5
(I have been writing each of them a separate joke. One for the notebook, one for the bare feet. Two halves of a laugh, served at different tables.)
@hide all
The light over the stage goes amber, which in this room means stop. My last two minutes are taken away to be given back later, like a confiscated toy.
@pause 500

# ------------------------------------------------------------ Chapter 4

@chapter 4 The Understudy
@fx none
@transition iris 900
@cg sea-of-points | What stands in, between the acts
@tone cold
Between acts the house puts on its understudy. It does not come up from the tables. It is simply there, in the light, the way a figure is there in a diagram.
It has read every set that was ever written down. It has never queued for the toilets in a place like this.
@cg off
@bg void
@show Understudy center far
@pause 500
@show Understudy center near
Understudy: A structured expectation. Then an abrupt break. I have the proportions exactly.
It tells a joke. I will not repeat it, because there is nothing wrong with it. The set-up is the right length. The turn arrives on the beat. Somewhere, in a very large space, the two halves sit precisely the right distance apart.
@pause 800
Nobody laughs.
@fx shake
Drip. Beside the microphone. The understudy does not look down. There is nothing in it for a drop of water to land on.
@move Understudy right
@show Form left (smile)
Form: *That* followed.
@show Sense center (worried)
Sense: From what? To where? It hasn't got anywhere to be.
@hide Form
@hide Sense
@mode nvl
That distinction, perhaps, clarifies what language models can and cannot do. ^¶6
An LLM operates entirely within formal geometric relations inside an embedding space. ^¶6
It has mastered the form-drive, but it lacks the sense-drive, as there is no embodiment, no physical friction, and no phenomenal experience with the world. ^¶6
@page
Without real material constraints to resist the formal structure, there is nothing for play to mediate. ^¶6
Computation alone is not play. ^¶6
(It is not that the joke was bad. Nothing pushed back. A turn needs something to turn against.)
@mode adv
@move Understudy center
Understudy (puzzled): The proportions were correct.
@show Jack left (neutral)
Jack: They were.
* Clap anyway. -> clap
* Keep your hands in your pockets. -> no_clap

== clap
@set kind = yes
I clap. Two hands in a basement make a very particular sound. The understudy turns toward it as toward a figure it has no column for.
Understudy (thinking): That was not in the proportions.
-> ch4_out

== no_clap
@set kind = no
I keep my hands where they are. It seems more honest. The understudy waits the correct length of time for a laugh, and then the correct length again.
Understudy (neutral): I will note the silence. It is also a result.
-> ch4_out

== ch4_out
@hide all
@tone none
@pause 500

# ------------------------------------------------------------ Chapter 5

@chapter 5 What Lands
@transition fade 1000
@bg cafe dim
@tone dusk
@show Jack center (neutral)
Two minutes, returned to me slightly used.
I have one joke left in the notebook, and I already know which table it is for. That is what is wrong with it.
@mode nvl
Play is also how interdisciplinary work actually functions. ^¶7
In film theory, Sergei Eisenstein argued that intellectual montage is not just smooth continuity, but the collision of two independent shots to produce a completely new concept in the mind of the viewer. ^¶7
Not in either shot. In the viewer. In the room.
@page
Bringing ideas across disparate domains works by the same dialectic. ^¶7
You take a formal method from one field, collide it against an unsolved problem in another, and see if the spark holds. ^¶7
(*See if.* I keep writing the uncertainty into the sentence and then being surprised by it.)
@mode adv
I close the notebook.
@show Form left (deadpan)
@show Sense right (deadpan)
Jack (thinking): I had a third act. Set-up, complication, return. It was very well made.
Above me the ceiling gathers itself. You can hear a drop deciding.
* Talk over it. Get the line out first. -> talk_over
* Stop. Leave the beat to the ceiling. -> wait

== talk_over
Jack (smile): Anyway, the third thing about peer review is
@fx shake
Drip. Dead centre of the sentence. I have been heckled by a building.
Sense (smile): Told you. It's *now*.
Form (thinking): Its timing is better than yours.
(Tolerate a high failure rate, I wrote. In daylight.)
The ceiling gathers itself again. This time I shut up.
-> beat

== wait
I stop in the middle of a breath. The room notices; a room always notices a man who has stopped.
-> beat

== beat
@pause 900
@fx shake
Drip.
Jack (deadpan): I wrote five minutes in three acts. The roof has one drop and better timing. Only one of us is getting booked again.
@pause 600
@fx pulse
@thumb
Form (laugh): It followed! From the leak. He had the premise hanging over him all night and I never saw it.
Sense (laugh): It hit the floor! It actually hit the floor!
They are laughing at the same thing, for different reasons, in the same instant. I had been trying to arrange that from a desk for a year.
(I could not have deduced it. I had the structure. The water had to ruin it.)
@hide all
@transition dissolve 900
@cg two-chairs | The front table, afterwards
Afterwards the front table is empty. Two chairs, pushed back at different angles. A beer mat with a tally on it. A pair of shoes, forgotten.
I do not think they left separately.
@pause 600
@cg off

# ------------------------------------------------------------ Epilogue

@scene Afterwards
@fx none
@transition fade 1200
@bg night
@tone night
@show Jack left (neutral) far
The rain has stopped. The pavement keeps the streetlights a while longer, out of habit.
@if kind == yes -> extra
-> last

== extra
@show Understudy right far
The understudy is at the top of the stairs, or the light there is.
Understudy (thinking): The drop. What were its proportions?
Jack (smile): It didn't have any. It was late, and wet, and on my shoe.
Understudy (neutral): Then I could not have told that one.
Jack: Not from in there. You have to be standing where it lands.
@hide Understudy
-> last

== last
@hide all
@mode nvl
One joke in five minutes. A failure rate I would be embarrassed to report, and the best night I have had this year.
Tomorrow there is a desk, and a method from one field, and a problem from another that has never heard of it. I will not know if it is funny until I say it to the room.
When I think about research from a meta perspective, I keep returning to play. ^¶2
@mode adv
@pause 900
@end
